<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Iberia</h1><p class="st-meta">588 CE – 888 CE · capital Tbilisi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rule of presiding princes over eastern Georgia between the fall of its ancient monarchy and the rise of the Bagratids, a time of Arab conquest and church building.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> abolished the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia</a> in 580, the Georgian nobility was left without a king. In 588, with Byzantine backing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaram-i-of-iberia">Guaram I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaramid-dynasty">Guaramid</a> family was recognised as presiding prince, holding the Byzantine title of kouropalates, and the office passed among leading families for three centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The presiding princes, or erismtavari, were first among equals over regional nobles rather than true monarchs, balancing between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-i-of-iberia">Stephen I</a> built the Jvari church above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, a model for Georgian architecture. Arab armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 645, and by 736 the city became the seat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-tbilisi">Emirate of Tbilisi</a>, pushing princely power west and south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagratid</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-i-of-iberia">Ashot I</a> moved the family's base to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-iberians">Tao-Klarjeti</a> in the early 9th century. In 888 his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adarnase-iv-of-iberia">Adarnase IV</a> revived the royal title, creating the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-iberia.jpg" alt="Principality of Iberia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Ec.Domnowall (vectorisation SVG) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kartli_-_drosha_jvari.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>588 CE – 888 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tbilisi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guaram I, Stephen I, Ashot I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Georgia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Presiding Princes of Iberia, Erismtavari of Kartli</dd></dl></aside></div>
